## 1. Statement and important qualification about the meaning of the edge

Let the graph be the simple nearest-neighbor cubic torus of even side \(L\),
with all A sites occupied, \(n=|A|=|B|=L^3/2\), the full physical Gauss
space, and the supplied Hamiltonian

\[
h_g=\frac{g^2}{2\tau}D+\frac1{4\tau g^2}H_4,\qquad \tau>0.
\]

All limits below have this graph and \(\tau\) fixed. For degree six the torus
has \(L\ge4\). If \(L=2\) is included under the parents' **simple-graph**
convention, it is the degree-three cube. Six distinct directional edges on a
side-two periodic multigraph are a different premise and are not covered.

An explicit nonnegative integer matrix \(Q_L\), indexed by unordered pairs
of B sites, is defined in (4) below. Put
\(\Lambda_L=\lambda_{\max}(Q_L)\). This finite combinatorial matrix, and its
small symmetry quotient, specify the coefficient exactly for every fixed
admissible \(L\); no infinite electric truncation enters its definition. Put

\[
q_{0,L}=\begin{cases}
54,&L=2\text{ (simple cube)},\\
270n,&L=4,\\
321n,&L\ge6.
\end{cases}
\]

Writing \(e_0(g)\) and \(e_1(g)\) for the spectral infima in record sectors
\(N=n\) and \(N=n+2\), respectively, the reconstruction gives

\[
e_0(g)=-\frac{q_{0,L}}{2\tau g^2}+O_L(1),\qquad
e_1(g)=-\frac{\Lambda_L}{2\tau g^2}+O_L(1),
\]
\[
\boxed{e_1(g)-e_0(g)
 =-\frac{\Lambda_L-q_{0,L}}{2\tau g^2}+O_L(1).}\tag{1}
\]

The coefficient has a definite sign without any numerical eigenvalue
assumption:

\[
\Lambda_L-q_{0,L}\ge
\begin{cases}
5160-1380/(n-1)>0,& L=4,\\
6048-774/(n-1)>0,& L\ge6.
\end{cases}\tag{2}
\]

Thus the full one-pair sector has a *lower* small-\(g\) spectral infimum on
every degree-six torus in scope. The simple cube is an actual exception:
\(\Lambda_2=49\), so its relative coefficient is \(+5/(2\tau g^2)\).

The edge is also seen in a precise leading-order sense by an **original
resolved mark**, without replacing its output by a sector minimizer. Let
\(\Omega_{E_0}\) be any one fixed finite integer-electric, divergence-free
all-A-plus/B-empty basis vector. For either resolved sign and any allowed
edge, define the immediate normalized output

\[
\eta_j=B_j\Omega_{E_0}/\sqrt{z_a-1}.
\]

Let \(b_j(g)\) be the infimum of the support of its spectral probability
measure for \(h_g\), equivalently the bottom on its energy-cyclic subspace.
Then

\[
\boxed{\lim_{g\downarrow0}g^2b_j(g)=-\Lambda_L/(2\tau),\quad
\lim_{g\downarrow0}g^2[b_j(g)-e_0(g)]
       =-(\Lambda_L-q_{0,L})/(2\tau).}\tag{3}
\]

In addition, the limiting probability measure of \(g^2h_g\) in this actual
output has \(-\Lambda_L/(2\tau)\) in its support. These are statements about
support, not a mass point, typical energy, the mean, or ground-state
preparation. I do **not** establish that \(b_j(g)=e_1(g)\) exactly at each
nonzero \(g\). That would need an additional finite-\(g\) connectivity or
component-bottom argument. Equation (3) answers access to the leading edge
and does not conceal that distinction.

Here the original mark is applied to the specified fixed incoming basis
state. A mark after a prescribed positive first-event time generally has a
