## 2. Exact occupied-B and charge bookkeeping

Use A-to-B edge orientations, so the parent electric operator is

\[
D(q,E)=\sum_{a\in A,\ b\sim a,\ q_b=0} E_{ab}(E_{ab}-q_a).
\]

Every summand is nonnegative for integer fields and \(q_a=\pm1\).
Occupied B sites remove their incident summands. They are not replaced by
an ungated field Laplacian. The diagonal multiplication operator is
self-adjoint on its actual domain and finite-electric physical vectors are
a core. It may have unconfined directions.

Gauss law on this connected periodic graph gives \(\sum q=n\).
In \(P\), all n A sites are occupied. For \(N=n\), all are plus and B is
empty. For \(N=n+2\), precisely two B sites are occupied and precisely one
of the \(n+2\) occupied sites carries minus charge. Its location can be on A
or either occupied B site. The full finite matter dimension is therefore
\((n+2){n\choose2}\). Every such matter word admits an integer Gauss flow:
route its integer zero-sum divergence along a spanning tree. No admissible
one-minus word is omitted.

From the exact local-pair parent, on **all** of this \(P\) space,

\[
H_4=-2\sum_{\{u,v\}\subset A:\ N(u)\cap N(v)\ne\varnothing}
             (F_vF_uP)^*(F_vF_uP).
\]

The minus charge is transported by the same legal hops as the plus charge.
The matter outputs can interfere. This proof does not dephase them or extend
the first-sector field-only plaquette formula to the one-pair sector.

## 3. Reduction of the magnetic infimum to a finite flat-connection matrix

Choose one fixed integer Gauss solution \(E_q\) for each matter word. The
integer divergence-free cycle lattice is free of rank

\[
r=|\mathcal E|-|A\cup B|+1.
\]

Every allowed electric field is uniquely \(E_q+Ck\), \(k\in\mathbb Z^r\),
after a cycle basis is chosen. Fourier transformation gives the physical
sector as \(L^2(\mathbb T^r;\mathbb C^M)\). This includes harmonic cycles;
none is fixed, projected out or replaced by a pure field value. Finite
magnetic words become Laurent matrices. Write

\[
H_4(\theta)=-2K(\theta),\qquad
K(\theta)=\sum S_{uv}(\theta)^*S_{uv}(\theta)\ge0.
\]

All primitive hopping amplitudes at \(\theta=0\) are positive integers.
Consequently \(K(0)\) is entrywise nonnegative and
\(|K(\theta)_{\alpha\beta}|\le K(0)_{\alpha\beta}\). For any vector \(v\),

\[
\langle v,K(\theta)v\rangle
 \le\langle |v|,K(0)|v|\rangle
 \le\lambda_{\max}(K(0))\|v\|^2.
\]

Continuity in the finitely many torus variables, and smooth packets
concentrated near zero, show that the full magnetic spectral infimum is
exactly \(-2\lambda_{\max}(K(0))\). A point with flat connection is used to
identify the continuous matrix minimum; it is not itself a normalizable
field state.

For each overlapping A pair define, for distinct B sites \(b,d\),

\[
m_{uv}(\{b,d\})=
 1_{b\in N(u),d\in N(v)}+1_{d\in N(u),b\in N(v)}.
\]

Let \(J,J'\subset B\) have size two. The promised occupation matrix is

\[
\boxed{
Q_L(J',J)=\sum_{uv}\ \sum_{\substack{R\subset B,\ |R|=4\\
                                         R\supset J\cup J'}}
             m_{uv}(R\setminus J')m_{uv}(R\setminus J).
}\tag{4}
\]

This formula directly enumerates two outward hops into empty B sites,
followed by two inward hops. Equivalently introduce auxiliary hard-core
occupation creation operators \(\beta_b^*\) and
\(C_{uv}^*=\sum_{|D|=2}m_{uv}(D)\prod_{b\in D}\beta_b^*\).
Then \(Q_L=\sum C_{uv}C_{uv}^*\) on the two-B-occupation space. These
auxiliary occupation variables are a calculation, not a modified physical
instrument or an all-plus physical one-pair sector.

To relate (4) to the full colored matrix, fix an initial one-minus word and
follow a primitive four-hop path. Its legality depends only on occupancy.
The colors determine one final minus location, without changing its positive
flat-connection amplitude. Sum all final colored matrix entries with a
fixed B pair \(J'\). This gives exactly \(Q_L(J',J)\), independently of the
initial minus location. Thus the partition into B pairs is equitable.

For degree six \(Q_L\) is irreducible. Indeed a B token may move from \(x\)
to a vacant \(y\) sharing A neighbor \(u\), while the other token \(w\)
stays fixed: choose an A vertex \(v\ne u\) overlapping \(u\), and

\[
z\in N(v)\setminus\{x,w,y\}.
\]

There is such a \(z\) since \(v\) has six neighbors. The four hops

\[
u\to y,\quad v\to z,\quad z\to v,\quad x\to u
\]

are a permitted \(S_{uv}^*S_{uv}\) path. The B distance-two graph is
connected, and the two-token exclusion graph of a finite connected graph
with at least one vacant vertex is connected (move holes/tokens along a
spanning tree, inductively fixing a leaf). Thus these paths connect all B
pairs. On the simple cube, (4) is the \(6\times6\) matrix with diagonal 9
and every off-diagonal 8, so it is also irreducible.

Let \(v_J>0\) be the Perron vector of \(Q_L\). Give every colored word with
B pair \(J\) the component \(v_J\). By the equitable identity this strictly
positive full-fiber vector is an eigenvector of \(K(0)\) with eigenvalue
\(\Lambda_L\). For a nonnegative finite matrix a strictly positive
eigenvector has its spectral-radius eigenvalue: conjugation by its positive
diagonal entries yields a matrix with constant row sum. Therefore

\[
\lambda_{\max}(K(0))=\Lambda_L.
\]

This step checks the full one-minus physical fiber; it does not assume its
own irreducibility or discard color interference.

In the initial sector the same count gives

\[
q_{0,L}=\sum_{uv}\sum_{|D|=2}m_{uv}(D)^2
       =\sum_{uv}[z_uz_v-r_{uv}+r_{uv}(r_{uv}-1)],
\quad r_{uv}=|N(u)\cap N(v)|.
\]

This agrees with the scalar-plus-four-cycle formula in the parent, including
periodic four-cycles on the smaller tori.

## 4. The electric term and the \(O(1)\) variational remainder

Let \(\lambda\) denote \(q_{0,L}\) or \(\Lambda_L\), as appropriate. Since
\(D\ge0\), the magnetic bound gives \(h_g\ge-\lambda/(2\tau g^2)\).

For the reverse inequality, in the physical Fourier representation take
the real positive flat-fiber Perron vector, multiplied by a normalized real
even smooth bump of width \(g\) centered at \(\theta=0\). For sufficiently
small \(g\) it is supported within a single torus coordinate chart. This is
a physical vector smooth in every cycle variable, hence it has all finite
moments of \(1+\sum_e E_e^2\) and belongs to \(D(D)\).

The occupied-B \(D\) is a quadratic differential expression with finitely
many charge-dependent coefficients and fixed integer Gauss offsets. Thus
its expectation in these bumps is \(O_L(g^{-2})\), without a lower coercive
bound. The expectation of \(H_4(\theta)\) in the real fiber vector is an
even real finite Fourier polynomial; the first derivative at zero vanishes.
Its difference from \(-2\lambda\) is \(O_L(|\theta|^2)\). Consequently the
trial energy is

\[
-\lambda/(2\tau g^2)+O_L(1).
\]

This proves (1). The trial argument only uses smooth vectors. In particular,
it does not assert that arbitrary charge-changing finite shifts preserve the
operator domain of a degenerate occupied-B electric \(D\). No compact
resolvent, normalizable ground state, or harmonic-sector gap is assumed.

