## 6. Why the actual resolved mark sees the leading edge

For a selected edge \((a,b)\) and sign \(\sigma\), the original parent
mark is \(B_j=Pj_{(a,b,\sigma)}F_aP\). Applied to the specified initial
all-plus basis word, its branches are indexed by
\(z\in N(a)\setminus\{b\}\). The old plus charge moves from \(a\) to \(z\);
the formation refills \(a\) with \(\sigma\) and places \(-\sigma\) at \(b\).
In the A-to-B orientation the electric changes are

\[
\Delta E_{az}=-1,\qquad \Delta E_{ab}=\sigma.
\]

Their divergence is exactly the matter-charge change. Different \(z\)
have orthogonal matter words, so \(\|B_j\Omega_{E_0}\|^2=z_a-1\).
Both resolved signs give nonzero physical outputs. Neither sign's output
is replaced by an arbitrary field-optimized vector.

Under the Gauss Fourier representation, the normalized actual output is a
finite Laurent vector \(\eta_j(\theta)\). At zero angle its \(z_a-1\)
components have positive amplitudes \(1/\sqrt{z_a-1}\). This remains true
for arbitrary fixed integer \(E_0\), arbitrary integer reference flows and
harmonic electric offsets: all their Laurent phases equal one at zero.
Its inner product with the strictly positive lifted Perron vector in
Section 3 is therefore strictly positive.

Let \(V=H_4/(4\tau)\) in the one-pair sector and
\(v_*=-\Lambda_L/(2\tau)\). For any \(\epsilon>0\), choose a continuous
nonnegative function \(f\), supported inside
\((v_*-\epsilon,v_*+\epsilon)\), with \(f(v_*)>0\). At \(\theta=0\),

\[
\langle\eta_j(0),f(V(0))\eta_j(0)\rangle>0.
\]

Finite-dimensional continuous functional calculus makes this strict
inequality persist on a nonempty open torus neighborhood. Integration with
the full Haar measure gives
\(\langle\eta_j,f(V)\eta_j\rangle>0\). Combined with \(V\ge v_*\), this
proves that \(v_*\) is in the support of the actual output's magnetic
energy law. It need not be an atom; a single point of field-angle space has
zero Haar measure.

Now set

\[
A_g=g^2h_g=V+\frac{g^4}{2\tau}D.
\]

These are self-adjoint bounded perturbations of the diagonal \(D\). For
every finite-electric vector \(\phi\) and nonreal \(z\),

\[
(A_g-z)^{-1}(V-z)\phi-\phi
=-\frac{g^4}{2\tau}(A_g-z)^{-1}D\phi\longrightarrow0.
\]

The set \((V-z)\mathcal F\) is dense because \(V-z\) is bounded invertible
and the finite-electric vectors \(\mathcal F\) are dense. Uniform
resolvent bounds extend the convergence to the whole Hilbert space. Thus
\(A_g\to V\) in strong resolvent sense. Continuous compactly supported
spectral test functions converge strongly; equivalently the probability
measures in the fixed vector \(\eta_j\) converge weakly to its \(V\)-law
(the limit has total mass one, giving tightness).

In particular the positive expectation of \(f(V)\) above implies positive
expectation of \(f(A_g)\) for all sufficiently small \(g\). Hence

\[
v_*\le \inf\operatorname{supp}\mu_{\eta_j,A_g}
       <v_*+\epsilon
\]

eventually, proving (3). This method requires neither compact electric
resolvent nor a proof that a birth connects every Gauss/winding component
at fixed \(g\).

When \(L\ge4\), the strict inequality \(\Lambda_L>q_{0,L}\) permits a
fixed scaled-energy window strictly below \(-q_{0,L}/(2\tau)\) that has
positive limiting probability in the actual output. The argument establishes
positivity at each fixed graph, not a useful uniform probability, a local
detector efficiency, or concentration near the edge.

## 7. Independent controls and their exact limitations

`occupation_spectrum.py` constructs the simple graph, \(m_{uv}\) and the
integer occupation matrix without importing any parent or author program.
To avoid forming a huge dense matrix, it implements an exact normal-order
identity equivalent to (4). Write

\[
A=\sum_{uv}M_{uv}^2,\quad s_i=A_{ii},\quad
G(J',J)=\sum_{uv}m_{uv}(J')m_{uv}(J).
\]

On a row \(J=\{x,y\}\), start with diagonal \(q_{0,L}-s_x-s_y\);
add \(A_{xz}\) for every allowed one-token move \(x\to z\ne y\), and
likewise for \(y\). Finally add \(G(J',J)\) if \(J',J\) are equal or
disjoint, and subtract it if they overlap in exactly one site. For example,
the latter removes the forbidden spectator contribution from the sum over
the common fourth B site. On the diagonal this gives

\[
Q(J,J)=q_{0,L}-s_x-s_y+\sum_{uv}m_{uv}(xy)^2.
\]

All constructed row entries are checked nonnegative. The code checks exact
orbit balance, multiple translated/oriented rows in each orbit (5, 25, 45
and 90 rows for \(L=2,4,6,8\)), the uniform trial formula, and exact
rational spectral bounds. The symmetry argument above establishes the
remaining orbit reduction; the finite sample is its control, not a substitute.

